  "body": {
    "id": "thm:honest-family-confidence",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Under the independent Gaussian sampling design, uniformly over \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_K\\), \\(\\Pr_{\\theta}[\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{causal}}(\\theta)\\subseteq\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{out},n}\\text{ and }h(\\theta)\\in\\mathcal H_n]\\ge1-\\alpha\\).  The region \\(R_n\\cap\\Theta_K\\) is nonempty for every sample, and a rule reports a classification only when \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) is a singleton has probability at most \\(\\alpha\\) of reporting a classification different from \\(h(\\theta)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "theta",
      "ThetaK",
      "Fcausal",
      "Fout",
      "n",
      "h",
      "Hn",
      "Rn",
      "alpha"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:independent-environment-samples",
      "def:augmented-family-region",
      "thm:sharp-target-fiber",
      "lem:measurable-certified-selection"
    ],
    "justification": "Family coverage must retain structural uncertainty and remain well-defined when the raw covariance region misses the nonclosed model.",
    "gap": "Strieder et al. retain graph uncertainty by test inversion and Romano--Shaikh cover identified sets, but neither gives always-nonempty coverage of a discontinuous discrete target family under exact-rank covariance restrictions.",
    "consumer": "A Perturb-seq or Sci-Plex audit can abstain near collisions while maintaining finite-sample coverage of all effective target explanations under the stated covariance model.",
    "proof_tex": "For a fixed environment suppress the index \\(e\\), write\n\\(\\widehat\\Sigma=n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^nY_iY_i^{\\mathsf T}\\), and diagonalize\n\\(\\Sigma=O\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_d)O^{\\mathsf T}\\).  Writing \\(Y=O\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{\\sigma_j})Z\\) with independent standard normal coordinates, differentiation of the one-dimensional moment generating function gives\n\\(\\mathbb E Z_j^2=1\\), \\(\\mathbb E Z_j^4=3\\), and independence gives\n\\[\n \\mathbb E(Y_jY_kY_lY_m)\n =\\Sigma_{jk}\\Sigma_{lm}+\\Sigma_{jl}\\Sigma_{km}+\\Sigma_{jm}\\Sigma_{kl}. \\tag{24}\n\\]\nConsequently, independence across observations and a direct sum over matrix entries yield\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_{\\Sigma}\\lVert\\widehat\\Sigma-\\Sigma\\rVert_F^2\n =\\frac1n\\sum_{j,k}\\operatorname{Var}(Y_jY_k)\n =\\frac1n\\left\\{(\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma)^2+\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma^2)\\right\\}. \\tag{25}\n\\]\nUnder \\(\\Sigma\\preceq MI_d\\), the right side multiplied by \\(n\\) is at most\n\\(M^2(d^2+d)=M^2d(d+1)\\).  Summing (25) over the independent environments proves, uniformly over \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_K\\),\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_{\\theta}\\lVert\\widehat\\Sigma-\\theta\\rVert_w^2\n \\le(E+1)M^2d(d+1)=V_{\\max}. \\tag{26}\n\\]\nMarkov's inequality and \\(c_{\\alpha}^2=V_{\\max}/\\alpha\\) therefore give\n\\[\n \\Pr_{\\theta}\\!\\left(\\lVert\\widehat\\Sigma-\\theta\\rVert_w\\le c_{\\alpha}\\right)\n \\ge1-\\alpha. \\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nOn the event in (27), the true tuple satisfies the covariance box constraints and belongs to \\(W_n\\), so\n\\(\\theta\\in R_n\\cap\\Theta_K\\).  The sharp-fiber theorem gives\n\\(\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{causal}}(\\theta)=\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{LDL}}(\\theta)\\).  Since the latter family is one of the terms in the union defining \\(\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{out},n}\\), and \\(h(\\theta)\\) is one of the values defining \\(\\mathcal H_n\\),\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{causal}}(\\theta)\\subseteq\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{out},n},\n \\qquad h(\\theta)\\in\\mathcal H_n. \\tag{28}\n\\]\nCombining (27)--(28) proves the asserted uniform joint coverage.\n\nFor every sample, \\(\\text{lem:measurable-certified-selection}\\), with \\(\\epsilon=1\\), gives\n\\(\\widetilde\\theta_n=s_1(\\widehat\\Sigma)\\in\\Theta_K\\).  Since this point is included in \\(R_n\\), the intersection \\(R_n\\cap\\Theta_K\\) is never empty.  The selector is Borel measurable.  Moreover, membership of any fixed target set or fixed value of \\(h\\) in the reported objects is an existential statement over the semialgebraic certificate and region constraints; cylindrical elimination makes each such event Borel.  Thus the finite-valued reporting rule is measurable.\n\nFinally, suppose the rule reports a singleton classification different from \\(h(\\theta)\\).  On the event in (27), (28) puts \\(h(\\theta)\\) in \\(\\mathcal H_n\\), so a singleton \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) could only equal \\(\\{h(\\theta)\\}\\).  An incorrect report is therefore contained in the complement of (27), whose probability is at most \\(\\alpha\\).  This proves the finite-sample honesty statement.",
